WA-2025-HB1570.summary:
PECBA applies to all non-academic student employees who are enrolled in an academic or certificate program at Western Washington University (Western) and includes non-academic student employees in the following job classifications:
• advertising assistant;
• community service center coordinator;
• computer assistant, except computer assistants employed in the student technology center;
• crafts lab assistant;
• customer service representative;
• event assistant;
• media assistant;
• percussion area coordinator;
• program support staff;
• program support and committee student representative;
• writer and editor;
• Viking Union building manager;
• resident advisors;
• apartment advisors; and
• inclusion assistant.

The bill does not include specified higher education and state employees that are already covered under existing collective bargaining law. Executive employees, managers, executive assistants, confidential employees, and employees who assist certain assistant attorneys general are excluded from the bill.

An employee is considered enrolled during campus academic breaks, including any winter, spring, or summer break, if the employee is employed over the course of the campus academic break and is enrolled in an academic or certificate program in the quarter or semester immediately preceding, or subsequent to, the campus academic break.

The scope of bargaining does not include:
• the ability to terminate the employment of any individual if the individual is not meeting academic requirements;
• the amount of tuition or fees, except tuition and fee remission and waiver is within the scope of bargaining;
• the academic calendar; and
• the number of students to be admitted to a particular class or class section.

The compensation provisions in a collective bargaining agreement must not exceed the amount or percentage established by the Legislature. Western may provide additional compensation that exceeds the amount provided by the Legislature.

WA-2025-SB5937.summary:
Alternative Key Requirements .

Upon request, a landlord must offer a tenant an alternative key that does not use biometric identifier information or a software application operated on the tenant's mobile phone or similar electronic device. Examples of alternative keys include, but are not limited to, key fobs, key cards, physical keys, other forms of physical access, or keypads or similar devices that use manual entering of a coded sequence of numbers, letters, or both.

"Biometric identifier information" means a physiological, biological, or behavioral characteristic that is used to identify, or assist in identifying, an individual including, but not limited to:
• a retina or iris scan;
• a fingerprint;
• a voiceprint; or
• a scan or record of a palm, hand, or face geometry.

Smart Access Systems .

Smart access system data collection and privacy policy requirements are added to the RLTA.

"Smart access system" means any system that uses electronic or computerized technology, such as a radio frequency identification card, a mobile phone application, biometric identifier information, or any other digital technology that is designed to gather information in order to grant entry to a building containing a dwelling unit, its elevators, its common areas, or to an individual dwelling unit. A smart access system does not include methods of entry that solely rely upon a keypad or similar device that uses the manual entering of a coded sequence of numbers, letters, or both. "Smart access building" means a building containing a dwelling unit that uses a smart access system.

Data Collection.

A smart access building landlord or third party may collect only the minimum amount of authentication data and reference data necessary to enable the use of a smart access system for the purpose of granting a user access to a building, its elevators, its common areas, or to a user's dwelling. "User" means a tenant of a smart access building.

"Authentication data" means the data generated or collected at the point of authentication in connection with granting a user entry to a smart access building, common area, or dwelling unit through such building's smart access system, excluding data generated or collected by a video, audio, or camera system that is used to monitor entrances but not grant entry.

"Reference data" means the information against which authentication data is verified at the point of authentication by a smart access system in order to grant a user entry to a smart access building, dwelling unit, or common area.

